From 348ba7f6a94c9900c1916161491d90427f60dc93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 02:58:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(kanban): pickup counts its own picks against a
 provider's pacing limit

Found on worker-3, 2026-11-15 ~21:30Z (t_3b968070eaa23b18, fixed in t_af7d8fd97219c888).

Defect: `_evaluate` asked `pickup_hold()` once per candidate, and each call compared the provider's admit
limit with this machine's *running* count only. The cards the same pass had already picked were not
counted. With anthropic paced at limit 1, running 0, every anthropic candidate looked runnable, so one pass
copied a full batch of 10. Local dispatch started one and pace-held nine, which were evicted and copied
again the next tick (agent.log: `picked up 10 card(s)` every tick beside `dispatcher stuck ... llm_pacing=9`).
A card pinned to the machine below that batch was then recorded `budget_exhausted` ("pickup batch full
this tick") although spawn slots were free, so the pickup-stall alarm filed "Pickup queue full" and sent
diagnosis the wrong way.

Five whys:
1. Why was the pinned card budget_exhausted? The batch was full of anthropic copies.
2. Why was the batch full of them? Every one passed the pacing prediction.
3. Why did every one pass? Each was compared against running=0 < limit=1.
4. Why running=0 for the tenth card? The count reads running runs only; the nine picks earlier in the
   same pass are not runs yet.
5. Why was that not counted? `pickup_hold` had no notion of a pass; it predicted one card in isolation.

Fix: `_evaluate` keeps `provider -> cards picked this pass` and passes it to `_paced` -> `pickup_hold` ->
`_verdicts`, which compares `running + picked` with the limit. `_verdicts` reports which limited provider
admitted a card; once the card is actually picked (not guard-held, not skipped), that provider's count
goes up. A further card of a spent provider is skipped as `other` / `llm_paced: anthropic paced (limit 1,
running 0, 1 picked this pass)`, takes no batch room, and a pinned card below it reads `llm_paced`, never
`budget_exhausted`. An unlimited provider (admit limit None) reserves nothing. The claim-time `hold()`
path is unchanged (no `picked` argument).

Policy decision (card asked to decide, not hard-partition): no extra preference for machine-pinned cards
over pool cards for the paced slot in this change. The existing ranking deliberately lets explicit pool
urgency beat a fresh pin while age protects pins (test_kanban_pickup_pinned_first), and a score bonus here
would override that intent. With the mislabel fixed the pinned card's wait now reads as pacing, which is
the gate that actually holds it.

Tests:
- New tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_pickup_pacing_headroom.py: limit 1 / running 0 with 5 same-provider pool
  cards and a pinned one below copies exactly one; the pinned one is `other`/`llm_paced`, never
  `budget_exhausted` (also with a full batch); spent headroom leaves batch room for other providers; a
  running card shares the limit; `pickup()` copies only the one card. RED before (4 fail: 5-6 copies).
- test_kanban_pickup_paced_window: its "open" anthropic pool used factor 1.0, which is the open ramp
  (limit 1 on an idle host), so its 11-card window only passed because of this bug. It now uses
  OPEN_RAMP_FACTOR (no limit), matching the fixture's stated intent.
